1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:05,000
Module 4, Video 4.6: Put a Real Data Source on the Bus, the Giga Case

2
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:12,180
Right. So our objective here is taking Giga's real school data through a bronze, silver, and gold pipeline onto the bus,

3
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:15,480
uh, as a worked exchange you can copy for your sector.

4
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:21,620
So moving from a theoretical method into a live deployment, well, it usually reveals planning gaps.

5
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:28,120
How do we transition from a high-level theoretical method to a practical, actionable template for your ministry?

6
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:31,320
Well, we rely on an initiative called Giga.

7
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:32,400
Okay. Um,

8
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:39,160
Giga is a joint effort by the ITU and UNICEF, and they're mapping the world's schools and their connectivity.

9
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:47,440
Right. And because they publish open APIs alongside real live data, they provide a, you know, a complete concrete template.

10
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:54,380
We use this as our base model because it demonstrates every step of the reference architecture in practice, just ready to be copied.

11
00:00:55,140 --> 00:00:58,200
Yeah, but simply having an API doesn't guarantee data usability.

12
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:05,360
If a ministry intends to build a pipeline, what precise published materials does Giga provide to actually structure that data?

13
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:08,780
Sure. So Giga supplies five specific components.

14
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:09,380
First,

15
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:12,920
uh, open APIs that you can actually call to retrieve the data.

16
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:19,060
Got it. Second is the school master schema, which dictates the required fields describing each school.

17
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:25,540
Third, the QoS, or quality of service schema, that standardizes how internet quality is reported.

18
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:33,220
Makes sense. Yep. Fourth, they provide GeoJSON, which is the standard format for expressing exactly where the school sits geographically.

19
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:41,360
Uh-huh. And finally, it uses ISO 3166-1 alpha three, which enforces three-letter country codes for indexing.

20
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:46,820
Okay. Those schemas define the target structure, but incoming data feeds inevitably contain errors.

21
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:49,640
It operates much like quarantine protocols at customs.

22
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:54,280
You cannot let goods enter the domestic supply chain until they've been inspected, cleared, and stamped.

23
00:01:54,740 --> 00:02:02,780
So if a school was entered twice, pumping that unrefined source data directly into a national registry would corrupt downstream systems.

24
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:08,220
How does this raw data systematically transform into a registry other agencies can trust?

25
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:12,520
Right. It moves through a strict pipeline with three distinct stages.

26
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:15,280
So number one is bronze. Bronze, okay. Yeah.

27
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:19,120
This is the raw data exactly as received, kept entirely untouched.

28
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:23,280
We store this for audit purposes, ensuring a persistent record of the original input.

29
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:25,200
Then number two is silver.

30
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:27,380
And silver is where the cleaning happens.

31
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:30,340
Exactly. This is the cleaned and validated version.

32
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:33,820
It actively bounces the bronze data against a semantic map.

33
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:42,460
If the schema demands a three-letter ISO country code, um, and a record provides a four-letter string, the pipeline physically rejects it here.

34
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:46,080
Bad or duplicate records are flagged, forcing conformance.

35
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:55,220
I see. And then number three is gold. This is the published authoritative version containing only verified records that other services consume.

36
00:02:55,820 --> 00:03:02,620
Right. So storing the bronze stage securely while exposing only the gold stage implies a highly controlled network environment.

37
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:08,440
How does this three-stage sequence integrate with the broader reference architecture to ensure secure data exchange?

38
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:11,920
So the flow is continuous but heavily compartmentalized.

39
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:16,320
Data lands as bronze, is cleaned to silver, and published as gold.

40
00:03:16,620 --> 00:03:21,200
Okay. That gold layer service is then strictly described by an OpenAPI contract.

41
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:25,520
From there, it is registered as an X-Road service description.

42
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:27,300
Uh, X-Road acts as the tunnel.

43
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:31,920
Right. X-Road acts as a secure encrypted tunnel between government agencies.

44
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:37,160
The data is secured across three isolated trust zones, preventing unauthorized internal access,

45
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:42,540
and is made lawful by applying a data protection envelope to ensure privacy compliance.

46
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:50,460
Wow. Okay. And this exact integration template applies whether the source is schools, health facilities, or a pharma registry.

47
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:58,040
Well, if the template is identical across sectors, an official might ask why they shouldn't simply build this pipeline using their own program's data from day one.

48
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:01,080
Like, why invest time practicing on Giga's data?

49
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:06,500
Because building a pipeline directly on live data risks disrupting active systems.

50
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:15,200
Mistakes on the national registry lead to systems that are fragmented, siloed, duplicated, rekeyed, locked into one supplier, and

51
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:16,380
costly to change.

52
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:17,360
Yeah, that makes sense.

53
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:18,120
But

54
00:04:18,899 --> 00:04:22,760
Giga is public, real, and fully standards aligned.

55
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:28,520
Practicing on Giga means any architectural mistake has zero consequences.

56
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:36,380
It provides a forgiving environment to run the pattern flawlessly, proving your infrastructure works before you touch a live national registry.

57
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:36,780
Right.

58
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:40,060
The Giga case is the method made concrete.

59
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:44,920
By learning this pipeline, bronze, silver, gold, contract, bus,

60
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:51,840
on public forgiving data, you gain a template to copy onto your own national registries with confidence.

61
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:54,120
Sources are in the video description.
